  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced national capacity to develop and implement a gender sensitive policy and strategies to promote employment creation</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Unemployment continues to be high despite employment data showing a decline in unemployment to 32.1 in Q3 of 2024, down from a two-year high of 33.5%% in Q3 of 2023. Youth aged 15-24 years and 25-34 years continue to have the highest unemployment rates at 58,0% and 38,3% respectively. The rate of youth not in education, employment and training continues to be of concern with the rate of female NEETs higher than that of their male counterparts. The mismatch of skills supplied and those demanded by the labour market is a contributing factor to unemployment, and the need for a policy environment that encourages lifelong learning to keep pace with emerging skills requirements. Public Employment Services require strengthening to prepare young work seekers for the labour market. The shift towards a greener economy necessitates a workforce with requisite skills. The methodology for the development of the Critical Skills List needs to be reviewed to identity skills required to contribute to economic growth and generation of employment opportunities. Digital skills training for youth and for educators presents an opportunity to increase the employability of young people in the growing digital and platform economy. 
The strategy will focus on capacity building support for modernized public employment services; the promotion of quality apprenticeships and lifelong learning and a costing tool for lifelong learning; continued capacity support towards the accreditation of digital skills training curriculum and training of educators in digital skills; technical, and capacity support towards identifying requiring green skills with a view to upskill and reskills workers at risk of losing jobs. Technical and capacity support will also be directed towards national efforts at reviewing the methodology for determining a national critical skills list required by the South African economy.</narrative>
  </description>
